================================================================================
 ABI v1 SECTION 3: THE LIFECYCLE, BUILT AND EXERCISED
 And what its one rule costs on the hot path.
================================================================================

CONFIGURATION (R7, R14)
  rustc 1.94.1 (e408947bf 2026-03-25), release, lto=false, PIE
  incumbent  prost 0.14.4, through the path tonic-prost actually calls
             (`Message::encode` / `Message::decode`; see
             logs/rust/stage5-pull-decode.log for the reading of
             tonic-prost 0.14.6's src/codec.rs that establishes it)
  linkage    shared library (ak-core as a cdylib), both arms, confirmed from ldd
  machine    4 vCPU Intel Xeon @ 2.10GHz, shared container
  guard      ABI v1 section 5's accessor guard ON throughout.  Not to be confused
             with `init-guard`, which is section 3's separate rule and is the arm
             below.
  arms       one source tree, two builds:
               default                 entry points do not check for `ak_init`
               --features init-guard   every entry point checks, and returns
                                       AK_ERR_UNINITIALIZED if it has not run
             Separate CARGO_TARGET_DIRs, so neither build is the other's stale
             artifact.
  R4         The two builds cannot share a process, so the comparison carries the
             in-process control R4 requires: `prost` and `core-native` are in
             every run and NEITHER has the guard.  If they move between the
             builds, the machine moved and the guard's column is not readable.

WHY SECTION 3 HAD NEVER BEEN BUILT, WHICH IS ITSELF THE FINDING
  The codec half needs none of it.  So no slice built it, and section 3's central
  claim -- "every other entry point requires `ak_init` to have returned
  successfully, THE CODEC INCLUDED" -- had never been exercised anywhere in this
  branch.  `findings/rust.md` lists it under "the specified surface that is not
  built" and every other slice's list says the same.

WHAT IS BUILT HERE AND WHAT IS NOT
  Built and exercised: `ak_init` with its options struct and flags; the `ak_err`
  out-parameter and its detail codes; the ABI-version check, made by the side
  that knows what it was generated against; idempotence under identical options,
  with AK_ALREADY_INITIALIZED as a SUCCESS; refusal on different options; a null
  options pointer; no `ak_shutdown`, by not existing; `ak_build_id`; the log
  bridge and AK_INIT_OWN_LOGGING; the panic hook and AK_INIT_NO_PANIC_HOOK; the
  AK_ERR_UNINITIALIZED guard on every entry point, behind a feature and priced;
  and the one-shot rule under eight racing threads.

  NOT built, and none of it is cheap to fake:
    * the rustls crypto provider installed by name.  This build does not link
      rustls at all; AK_INIT_NO_CRYPTO names the case a codec-only host is in,
      which is four of the five slices.
    * `tracing::set_global_default` and `log::set_logger`.  The bridge built here
      is the ABI's own `ak_log_fn`, which is what a C host can consume; wiring
      the two Rust crates to it is a different job.
    * configuration precedence (an explicit setter beats the environment, the
      environment beats the JSON handed to `ak_context_new`, that beats the
      defaults) and the `worker_threads` default.  Both belong to
      `ak_context_new` and `ak_runtime_opts`, which are the RPC half, and neither
      exists here.  **This is the behavioural half of section 3 and it remains
      unexercised** -- it is the half that turns `GrpcClient__AllowUnsafeConnection`
      into a silent certificate bypass if the order is inverted.

  A one-line install nobody has run is not evidence, which is why the list above
  is in the log rather than in a footnote.

WHY EVERY CASE IS ITS OWN PROCESS
  `ak_init` is one-shot per process by construction and there is no
  `ak_shutdown` to undo it, so a suite that ran two cases in one process would be
  measuring the second case against the first case's installs.  The binary
  re-runs itself with a case name and the parent reports each child's verdict.
  Two of the cases cannot exit at all -- they end in a process abort, on purpose,
  and the parent reads that from the exit status.

