================================================================================
 WHAT THE RUNS ABOVE ESTABLISH
================================================================================

1. SECTION 3's CENTRAL CLAIM IS NOW EXERCISED, AND IT HAD NEVER BEEN

   `uninit-first` calls a codec entry point before `ak_init`. With the guard on
   it returns **AK_ERR_UNINITIALIZED (-10)**, which is the first time anything in
   this branch has run that path.  With the guard off the same call succeeds, and
   the case says so rather than skipping: **section 3's rule is unenforced unless
   the guard is compiled in**, and a slice that never built it had no way to know.

   SCOPE, PRECISELY.  The guard is on all **47 emitted codec entry points** and
   the count is checked rather than assumed (`grep -c 'extern "C" fn ak_'` and
   `grep -c 'ak_init_ok'` over the emitted codec both return 47).  The **37
   hand-written entry points** in `ak-core/src/lib.rs` -- the contexts, the
   transcoders, the counters, the lifecycle itself -- are NOT guarded, and one of
   them must never be: `ak_fail` is the error channel and has to work when
   nothing else does.  Whether `ak_enc_ctx_new` should return null before
   `ak_init` is a design question and is not taken here.

2. THE STATE MACHINE BEHAVES AS WRITTEN

     ak_init(good opts)                 -> AK_OK (0), ak_initialized() -> 1
     ak_init(same opts again)           -> AK_ALREADY_INITIALIZED (1), a SUCCESS
     ak_init(different opts)            -> AK_ERR_INVALID_STATE (-8),
                                           detail AK_DETAIL_OPTS_DIFFER
     ak_init(abi_version = 8)           -> AK_ERR_ABI (-11),
                                           detail AK_DETAIL_ABI_MISMATCH,
                                           and ak_initialized() stays 0
     ak_init(NULL) and ak_init(NULL,NULL) -> AK_ERR_INVALID_STATE, not a crash

   **The idempotence rule holds under contention, not only under a second call.**
   Eight threads racing `ak_init`: exactly **1 AK_OK, 7 AK_ALREADY_INITIALIZED,
   0 other**, and **0 callers returned before the installs were visible** -- which
   is the half that matters, because "every other entry point requires `ak_init`
   to have returned successfully" is a claim about every caller and not about the
   first one.

3. TWO CASES FAILED FIRST AND BOTH FAILURES WERE THE SPECIFICATION WORKING

   a. **The core's panic hook does not see a Rust host's panics.**  The first
      draft panicked in host code and expected the hook to fire.  It does not: a
      cdylib carries its OWN copy of `std`, so the core's hook and the host's
      hook are two different globals.  Section 3 warns about exactly this
      mechanism one level up -- "two copies of the staticlib in one process
      either share Rust's globals or split-brain them with no warning" -- and
      here it is `std`'s globals rather than the core's.  It is not a defect: the
      hook is for panics in the CORE, which is where the host cannot see them.
      `host-panic-own-hook` keeps the contrast in the suite.

      Testing it needed a panic INSIDE the core, so the core now carries
      `ak_panic_test`, and the case's verdict is **"the host's sink got the
      message, then the process aborted"** -- which is the whole of what the hook
      buys.  It does not stop the panic.  `no-panic-hook` is the same case with
      the flag set and requires the opposite: no line, and still an abort.

   b. **The flags are a process-wide negotiation and the first caller wins.**
      `codec-after-init` failed because the case called `ak_init` with one flag
      set and `Ctx::new()` then called it with another.  That is section 3's "a
      second call with different options fails", working.  It is also a
      consequence the specification does not spell out: two independent
      components in one process that both initialise defensively -- which is the
      shape a host LIBRARY has, and which is why AK_ALREADY_INITIALIZED is a
      success at all -- cannot both choose, and the second gets a hard failure
      for asking rather than the first's settings.  **Two hosts loading one
      shared library is the normal case.**  It is now `two-components`, a case of
      its own, rather than a fixed test.  Raised and not taken: the alternatives
      (ignore the later flags, or merge them) each give up something section 3 is
      deliberately buying.

4. THE CODEC IS UNCHANGED BY ALL OF IT

   `codec-after-init` encodes P1.2 to 218,121 bytes, equal to prost's, and
   round-trips.  Section 5 of the transcript re-runs full byte identity across
   all four arms on the GUARDED build, because the guard adds a branch to every
   entry point and "additive" is a claim about behaviour.

5. WHAT THE GUARD COSTS: NOT TAKEN IN THIS COMMIT

   `gen/guardprice.sh` is the measurement and it is a SEPARATE script from
   `gen/lifecycle.sh` for a reason worth recording: it is four `bench` runs, it
   needs the box to itself, and this session lost a run to two benchmarks
   overlapping on one machine -- which is README section 11's "two benchmarks on
   one box corrupt each other silently: the numbers still come out", reproduced.
   The script now refuses to start if anything else is benchmarking.

   **No figure is quoted here until that run exists**, which is the rule the
   slice already lives by: a figure with no log behind it does not go in the
   report.  What can be said without the run, because it comes from the crossing
   counts rather than from a clock:

     The guard is a relaxed atomic load of one process-global word, a compare and
     a branch, at each of the 47 emitted entry points.  The counting build says
     how often those are reached: **1 forward crossing per decode on every
     payload**, and on encode 8 for P1.2 (1,000 elements) and 2,511 for P2.2 (500
     elements).  So the guard is per ENTRY POINT, and the entry points are per
     message or per chunk -- never per field.  A cost model would put it below the
     run-to-run spread on this machine, and the point of taking the measurement is
     that "would be" is not "is".

   The arms and the control are already in place for it: `--features init-guard`
   against the default build, with `prost` and `core-native` carried in both as
   the in-process control R4 requires, since neither of them has the guard.

6. WHAT THIS DOES NOT ESTABLISH

   * **The behavioural half of section 3 is untouched.**  Configuration
     precedence -- an explicit setter beats the environment, the environment beats
     the JSON handed to `ak_context_new`, that beats the defaults -- is the rule
     whose inversion turns `GrpcClient__AllowUnsafeConnection`, a pod-spec
     environment variable, into a silent certificate-verification bypass.  It
     belongs to `ak_context_new`, which is the RPC half, and neither exists here.
     Same for the `worker_threads` default, where the measured failure is a client
     that "does not look slow, it looks erratic".
   * **No crypto provider, no tracing or log crate bridge.**  Named above.
   * **The guard covers the 47 emitted entry points and not the 37 hand-written
     ones**, and `ak_fail` must never be guarded because it is the error channel.
     Whether `ak_enc_ctx_new` should refuse before `ak_init` is not decided here.
   * **The panic hook is a diagnostic and not a recovery.**  Every panic inside
     the core reaches an `extern "C"` frame and aborts; the hook's whole value is
     that the message gets to the host's log first.  Turning that into
     `AK_ERR_PANIC` -- which section 5's enum already reserves -- means catching
     the unwind at each entry point, which is a different and larger change and is
     not made here.
   * **`ak_build_id` is exported and nothing compares two of them.**  Section 3's
     case for it is two copies of the core in one process; this slice loads one.
   * One machine, one configuration.
